        <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
            <div class="faq-item">
                <div class="faq-q">Why does it show 60Hz when my monitor is 144Hz?</div>
                <div class="faq-a">
                    Most common causes: your OS display setting hasn't been switched
                    from the 60Hz default, you're using a cable that can't carry the
                    full bandwidth, browser hardware acceleration is off, or your
                    laptop is in a power-saving mode.
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <div class="faq-q">How accurate is a browser-based refresh rate test?</div>
                <div class="faq-a">
                    requestAnimationFrame is tied to your display's vsync, so under
                    normal conditions readings are accurate to within ±1–2Hz.
                    Background tabs, high CPU load, or OS-level frame pacing can
                    cause temporary dips — which is exactly what the stability grade
                    and frame-time chart are designed to surface.
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <div class="faq-q">What does the Stability Grade actually measure?</div>
                <div class="faq-a">
                    It's the percentage of frames that land within ±15% of your
                    session's median frame interval. A high average Hz with a low
                    grade usually means your setup is technically capable but not
                    consistently hitting that rate — worth investigating before you
                    trust the number on a spec sheet.
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <div class="faq-q">What's the difference between the VRR simulator and real G-Sync/FreeSync?</div>
                <div class="faq-a">
                    This simulator is a visual explainer, not a hardware test — your
                    browser can't control your monitor's actual variable refresh
                    window. It illustrates why VRR eliminates the stutter/tear
                    pattern you'd get from a fixed refresh rate when frame times
                    fluctuate.
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <div class="faq-q">Is the difference between 60Hz and 144Hz really noticeable?</div>
                <div class="faq-a">
                    Yes, very much so — most visible when scrolling quickly or
                    dragging windows. At 144Hz edges stay sharp; at 60Hz there's
                    clear motion blur. Improvements above 144Hz follow diminishing
                    returns for most people.
                </div>
